#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087606 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087779 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17b31ef7-a3d0-5315-8dbd-e92eafcce17f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Differential Equation Using Sinusoidal Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "c25573a9-f51a-5cf2-aa32-a343e96dd732" :question/math-academy-id "e-6612" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](images/6e/6e4e0c7cab964302651798fb741f1144f92f6e2e57e65101a7943111932fdbbd.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation has roots ![](images/a3/a397e1a4149f4b6081f65d95f04741e60e2bd31fca19d3f8e09ed0f76db581de.png) so the complementary solution is\n![](images/c9/c970482f98369bea187d9d95494395b7ddc3777c96c6f70928f5f7b2aae5f3b7.png)\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is ![](images/d0/d0cdd6ff1b44946ac1e910d23f7f40b83908992acbdba94329de63b7ee2c8f49.png) and ![](images/7f/7ffc37c1e99b8efa7829e449730a07f729b1089965446233266d7b7b26d0ce90.png) is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n![](images/9f/9f015e4ef6033cdc7ac1ff0de31ac36bf8d47b6fa702b773c32abc955bdb0d2f.png)\nwhere ![](images/67/6709a006285d5cd34ad5d0cc38c5a301ee5e685991d0a63609d33e3dc9273524.png) and ![](images/b4/b4c9f2e8e219faee88048f605f5ecf0957d84ed21896bbee9b25e7cb2953364d.png) are constants to be determined.\n\n**Caution:** The terms of the complementary solution are ![](images/c1/c1e8f4a9b683824e14a5b29e1bb1f23a90c03f90929c1ac8d04a3f2ba78d4c74.png) and ![](images/37/373d49e91c81adc100005e789dec85074254a9692d7858313966c886b589ec58.png) Although ![](images/16/16099b9989ef712e435b858d14cb006c5e62362412ae1b0e42e8025290dc644c.png) appears, ![](images/66/669043a118b0a018e699c203330af94918b1df9744a2d9c6e04109e4ca39499a.png) is not itself a term because it is multiplied by ![](images/6b/6b761e20295ebe1f6a5edf87ae5b7e480485dafbc0e623f4fab18efe2b8859c6.png)\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ![](images/63/6316c498377eadd30922f7f5ef6cfa1015cb3c059349bccf2aec7ca52ea5356e.png) we get\n![](images/48/48db0d80886317e03b0a772e8f36255308c9e1f8bac5824231981366a39e96f5.png)\n\nTo find the values of ![](images/60/6053d6cf8a3a4f931034631411cd2b07f16470ea835ae768c5783b33e0d18d55.png) and ![](images/f0/f00a9053a63ce88c1749e8ce7fb389b3618c6e3d4be6a4a03b7031759fe62940.png) we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n![](images/e4/e43bebbc4a12d780e880c4aa2fbb6f09f74e388163648858c95b0f2969d9168d.png)\n\nThe corresponding terms on both sides should have the same coefficients. Equating the coefficients of like terms, we get the following system:\n![](images/5f/5f5871fd2c97ac62a9143268e844d0b0b0482ef93e8841138b5d0e6d08bf911f.png)\n\nSolving this system gives ![](images/bb/bb5b3d4d7afe88b271c5feb2d48e9a5680c728cf6f33396eb4f226ad9bc4f3ff.png)\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n![](images/03/03049680a7f1934e4eb0a7031d73baf4789ee549f4495c563739dfe91ad1d19a.png)\nand the general solution is\n![](images/6d/6d839822be46648100d639e231ca1b4afe9460d4dec7b6a17c761f25475be381.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087607 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"}] :question/id #uuid "b11bf2d1-c79a-5308-bcda-109d217b14cb" :question/math-academy-id "e-6613" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 4y = 4\\sin 2x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation has roots $\\lambda = \\pm 2 \\textrm{i},$ so the complementary solution is \n\n$$\ny_c = A\\cos{2x} + B \\sin{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $4 \\sin 2x,$ and $\\sin 2x$ is a term in the complementary solution. So, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = x (\\alpha \\cos{2x} + \\beta \\sin{2x}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+2x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x), \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+4x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)+4x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)+4x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ -4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\ -4\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = -1$ and $\\beta = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = -x \\cos{2x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087615 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c07e202-40b7-5508-af3d-6a272ed0cbc2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "a23b7e25-1876-5a71-a638-18b5d548bd16" :question/math-academy-id "e-6788" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 4y = 4\\sin 2x\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x) = A \\cos{2x} + B \\sin{2x} - x \\cos{2x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 4y = 4\\sin 2x, \\qquad y(0) = 2 , \\ \\ y'(0) = -3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-0\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\ 2 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-2A\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2B\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x) \\\\  & =-2A\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x+2B\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y'(0)=-3$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =-2A\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+2B\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+2(0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\ -3 & =2B-1 \\\\ -2 & =2B \\\\ -1 & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=2$ and $B=-1,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1292269f-5e99-5354-924d-824238118a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order ODE When the Right-Hand Side and the Complementary Solution are Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e8ddebf-6077-5d0e-b67f-bb3db8ea7ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P{e}^{x}+Q{e}^{-x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333117} {:answer/id #uuid "1f0d7d81-8109-5f01-9b16-e65745dd234d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P{e}^{x}+Qx{e}^{x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333266} {:answer/id #uuid "2f963ca3-2ca5-57b5-adcc-81f8845db8d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333432} {:answer/id #uuid "b8112c53-2bff-538f-9ddb-b3214def130e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334601} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf178ed-b784-571f-bc50-2c14cc5ea4e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddf178ed-b784-571f-bc50-2c14cc5ea4e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334924} :answer-field/id #uuid "94cb94dc-1192-59f2-adf3-98882b35609c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbdef734-fa80-5bda-a3b6-8a4413098790" :question/math-academy-id "q-64276" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe particular solution of the inhomogeneous equation cannot be of the form $\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ because this is already included in the complementary function. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\ny_p = x (\\alpha \\cos x + \\beta \\sin x),\n]\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & =2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+x(-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+x(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -2\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-4$ and $\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}=P\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c07e202-40b7-5508-af3d-6a272ed0cbc2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e4850d-e1e4-58d4-912d-5473e23ec13b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{2x}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345362} {:answer/id #uuid "15281e8b-a08c-57d5-a710-3a326fe44fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345532} {:answer/id #uuid "80554915-2465-556b-8f4c-27dfd63254ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}-{e}^{-x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346474} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e5f1ae-963c-57ca-bc6d-5da2763061d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}+2{e}^{2x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347055} {:answer/id #uuid "f013c943-e9ae-5e8c-a7cd-0d6dd65bccde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{2x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15281e8b-a08c-57d5-a710-3a326fe44fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345532} :answer-field/id #uuid "4072f708-9f06-5dbb-8906-7670052d896b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c6cef86f-759f-5c1c-825d-36a431a1bfc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79633" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=-1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1$ or $\\lambda =2$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n-\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(-\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n(-3\\alpha -\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(\\alpha -3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha -\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ \\alpha -3\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,$ $\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow let us find constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B-1=0.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=-1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-A{e}^{-x}+2B{e}^{2x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=-1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n-A+2B=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B$:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=1 \\\\ -A+2B=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1,$ $B=0.$\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357575 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c07e202-40b7-5508-af3d-6a272ed0cbc2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a1f5d76-3f7f-547f-af3b-736832782bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=8{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355189} {:answer/id #uuid "687be201-c020-5689-bd00-8beafceda659" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355588} {:answer/id #uuid "9878ca06-9693-571d-8b14-5956b9faa289" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5x{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356013} {:answer/id #uuid "a556cb0e-8a4e-59e0-8fbb-13b3114952a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356131} {:answer/id #uuid "bbbc413d-5d01-571d-9160-1d9df074c837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbbc413d-5d01-571d-9160-1d9df074c837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356307} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bfc8ad6-cec8-59fa-9846-deca9a6219a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b33595b2-a9eb-5c04-8b11-c1d59ad62496" :question/math-academy-id "q-93829" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=-25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=-25\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,y(0)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=-3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3 & =A{e}^{2(0)}+B(0){e}^{2(0)}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\ -3 & =A-3 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2A{e}^{2x}+2Bx{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the derivative gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =2A{e}^{2(0)}+2B(0){e}^{2(0)}+B{e}^{2(0)}+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\ 5 & =2A+B+4 \\\\ 5 & =2(0)+B+4 \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=0$ and $B=1,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny=x{e}^{2x}+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$"}]}
